          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "POLYNOMIALS"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.875,
            0.104,
            0.899,
            0.119
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "17"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.138,
            0.902,
            0.236
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Note that 0 is the only zero of the polynomial "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x ^ { 3 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Also, from Fig. 2.7, you can see that 0 is the x-coordinate of the only point where the graph of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y = x ^ { 3 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " intersects the x-axis. Similarly, since "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x^{3}-x^{2}=x^{2}(x-1)"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " , 0 and 1 are the only zeroes of the polynomial "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "x ^ { 3 } - x ^ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " . Also, from Fig. 2.8, these values are the x - coordinates of the only points where the graph of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y = x ^ { 3 } - x ^ { 2 }"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " intersects the x-axis."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.095,
            0.241,
            0.901,
            0.282
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "From the examples above, we see that there are at most 3 zeroes for any cubic polynomial. In other words, any polynomial of degree 3 can have at most three zeroes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.096,
            0.285,
            0.9,
            0.344
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Remark :",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " In general, given a polynomial "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( x )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of degree n, the graph of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "y = p ( x )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " intersects the x-axis at atmost n points. Therefore, a polynomial "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( x )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " of degree n has at most n zeroes."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.097,
            0.356,
            0.897,
            0.397
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Example 1 :",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Look at the graphs in Fig. 2.9 given below. Each is the graph of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "{ \\overline { { y } } } \\equiv { \\overline { { p } } } ( x )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " where "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( x )"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " is a polynomial. For each of the graphs, find the number of zeroes of "
            },
            {
              "type": "equation_inline",
              "content": "p ( x )"
            }
          ]
        },
